    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d859">

                <pb facs="0911" n="813" sameAs="frus:frus1920v01/pg813"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">393.1123R27/14: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Crane</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Acting Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-12-13T17:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">December 13,
                                1920—5 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received December 13—11:58
                        a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>431. Am ordering Spiker<anchor n="59" xml:id="d859-f1"/> to Shanghai to ascertain
                    whether it is possible to secure arrest of Chang Ching Yao reported to be there
                    and have him tried by Mixed Court on the charge of refusing protection in
                    Reimert case. Definite knowledge of his whereabouts will at least make it
                    possible for the Minister to demand that he be apprehended and tried by the
                    Government of China. As Chinese officials are indifferent, some step of this
                    kind is positively necessary to prestige of the United States. Instructions are
                    desired.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Crane</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d859-f1" n="59" xml:id="d859-f1-content">
                <p>Clarence J. Spiker, vice consul, detailed to Peking.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
